#!/bin/sh
#
# MR-54 B1 — the commit check, as a MECHANISM.
#
# **Why this exists.** Three sessions running, a commit went out with a failure already on
# screen: vitest green, `tsc` red, and nobody ran `tsc`. MR-52 needed two follow-up commits
# for lint findings that were already printed; MR-53 committed `role: string` where a union
# was required and only found it a session later. Each time the rule was restated and each
# time it failed, so the rule is now a script instead of a sentence.
#
# **What it runs, and why it does not list the steps itself.** `scripts/ci-local.mjs` derives
# its step list by reading `.github/workflows/ci.yml`, for a reason that file argues at
# length: a hand-kept parallel list has already drifted three times in this repository, and
# it drifts in the direction of claiming MORE coverage than it has. So this hook names three
# steps by their COMMAND and lets that script find them. If `ci.yml` renames or removes one,
# `--only` matches zero steps and this hook FAILS rather than quietly checking less.
#
# **What it deliberately does NOT do.**
#
#   - It does not run the tests. `pnpm test` takes minutes and a hook that costs minutes is a
#     hook people pass `--no-verify` to, and a bypassed control is not a control. The three
#     steps here are the ones that have actually caught the failures, and they are the three
#     the brief named.
#   - It checks the WORKING TREE, not the index. That is the honest limitation: if you stage
#     broken code and then fix the file without staging it, this passes and the commit is
#     still broken. It is the standard trade (husky and lint-staged make the same one), and
#     the failure it was written for — a broken working tree, committed anyway — is caught.
#   - It cannot stop `git commit --no-verify`. Git allows the bypass by design. CI is still
#     the enforcement; this is the thing that tells you before the push.
#
# **Enable it once per clone:** `pnpm hooks:install`. Git does not read `core.hooksPath` from
# the repository — that is a security property, not an oversight, since a cloned repo must
# not be able to run code on `git commit` without being asked.

set -e

echo "pre-commit: typecheck, lint and format (MR-54 B1). Bypass with --no-verify."

if ! node scripts/ci-local.mjs --only=typecheck,lint,format:check; then
  echo ""
  echo "COMMIT REFUSED — a step CI runs has failed above."
  echo "Fix it, or commit with --no-verify and own the red build."
  exit 1
fi
